'''TESTAMENTUM DOMINI''' ("{{sc|Testament of our Lord}}"). Extracts from the book which bears this title, contained in an 8th-century MS. at Paris, were published by Lagarde in 1856 (''Reliquiae iuris ecclesiastici antiquissimae 80–89''); and a Latin fragment, edited by Dr Montague James, appeared in 1893 (''Texts and Studies'', i. 154). The whole book was first published in Syriac in 1899, with a Latin translation by Mgr Rahmani, the Uniat Syrian Patriarch of Antioch. His text is that of a 17th-century MS. at Mosul, the colophon of which says that the Syriac text was translated from the original Greek "''a Jacobo paupere,''" evidently James of Edessa, in {{sc|a.d.}} 687; but he makes use of other material, including an Arabic version made from a Coptic copy written in {{sc|a.d.}} 927. The Mosul MS. contains
